// recovery-state — premise library for recovery/repair_or_resume.
//
// WHEN TO USE: the planner is in recovery and you are about to repair state or
// resume. Prove the repair decision is sound before touching anything: the
// cause is pinned, the action kind is settled, and destructive actions carry
// user approval. Skip (not_applicable) only when recovery found nothing to
// repair (states already match).
//
// HOW TO USE (your program):
//   DOMAIN check
//   IMPORT "templates/recovery-state.vrf"
//   FACT recovery_state.repair safe_to_apply     // the claim under test
//   FACT recovery_state.repair is non_destructive   // or: is destructive
//   NOT  recovery_state.planner resumes_without_repair  // FACT or NOT, never omit
//   CHECK BIDIRECTIONAL
// Bind ports via `values`: {"cause_identified": true, ...}.
// A destructive repair additionally needs
//   FACT recovery_state.user approved_destructive
// and that fact must be REAL — the user actually said yes.
//
// INLINE RECIPE — hypotheses about what broke (copy into YOUR file):
//   // the engine's RETRACT list does the backtracking across guesses:
//   ASSUME crash during_merge
//   ASSUME NOT worktree was_touched_manually
//   ASSUME state_json is_stale
DOMAIN recovery_state

// -- external ports (bind via `values`) --
VAR cause_identified        // the expected-vs-actual divergence is explained
VAR expected_state_read     // state.json/plan records were actually read
VAR actual_state_inspected  // git/filesystem state was actually inspected
VAR repair_is_minimal       // the repair touches only what diverged

// -- exactly one repair kind, settled before acting; the closing rules
//    derive the negation so the other branch really closes --
PREMISE one_repair_kind:
    ONEOF
        repair is destructive
        repair is non_destructive
RULE destructive_closes_other:
    WHEN repair is destructive
    THEN NOT repair is non_destructive
RULE non_destructive_closes_other:
    WHEN repair is non_destructive
    THEN NOT repair is destructive

// -- destructive repairs demand explicit user approval --
PREMISE destructive_needs_approval:
    WHEN repair is destructive
    THEN user approved_destructive

// -- the claim: the repair is safe to apply --
PREMISE safe_needs_cause:
    WHEN repair safe_to_apply
    THEN cause_identified
PREMISE safe_needs_expected_read:
    WHEN repair safe_to_apply
    THEN expected_state_read
PREMISE safe_needs_actual_inspected:
    WHEN repair safe_to_apply
    THEN actual_state_inspected
PREMISE safe_needs_minimal:
    WHEN repair safe_to_apply
    THEN repair_is_minimal

// -- resuming without repairing is a claim too --
RULE resume_needs_alignment:
    WHEN planner resumes_without_repair
    THEN planner needs_state_match
RULE repair_path_no_alignment_claim:
    WHEN NOT planner resumes_without_repair
    THEN NOT planner needs_state_match
PREMISE state_match_gate:
    WHEN planner needs_state_match
    THEN planner states_match
